      "name": null,
      "problem": "Express $\\frac{1}{64}$ as a base raised to a negative exponent.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write $\\frac{1}{64}$ as a power with a negative exponent.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{64} & =\\frac{1\\times 1\\times 1}{4\\times 4\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\times \\frac{1}{4}\\times \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{1}{4}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}={4}^{-3}\n$$",
